   </h2>=0D=0A                            <p class=3D"corner-intro">A colle=
ction of web design and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Ch=
ris Coyier.</p>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A             =
             <h3>Type</h3>=0A<p><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/=
a/dqcD6hb310ziwTKfOpxiYQ~~/AABEfhA~/021K8pUgTm5o5u-OZARsz2dlvsRtRmKsTW55apd=
QNMmtkCiOB5JqAbdrds0UAAUEFtJLXduct1XGgGVjZQ60vWr9YL9GpprvRZE7pwxTaTKQ7ch1nA=
zg6UoH5TbZqOCW4WOJwixWVHVZe0JXzLXghvRL61NxTqxDHniQYy7JC_U~">Jake Archibald =
writes that we should just <em>not use</em> footnotes on the web</a>. Not u=
se footnotes=3F! I love a good footnote. Or at least, I thought I did. It=
=E2=80=99s where you can stick some information that provides a bit of extr=
a context, but would be distracting if smashed into the main text. They can=
 even change the tone, like how you can insert a joke where it might not wo=
rk otherwise.</p>=0A<p>But it would be hard to read Jake=E2=80=99s full-thr=
oated argument against them on the web. They really do jostle you around th=
e page awkwardly, simply because of the nature of the web and typical desig=
ns. Jake=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cjust use some damn parentheses=E2=80=9D is slig=
htly tongue-in-cheek but not without merit.</p>=0A<p>Personally, I like the=
 ideas that take more advantage of the unique powers of the web. Specifical=
ly the alternative that is:</p>=0A<ol>=0A<li>An <code>&lt;aside popover&gt;=
</code> opened/closed entirely with HTML attributes.</li>=0A<li>Positioned =
with anchor positioning</li>=0A</ol>=0A<p><img src=3D"https://production-co=
depen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-cgi/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90=
,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/example_popover.png" alt=3D"A popover 'footnot=
e' tooltip on hover. " width=3D"400"/></p>=0A<p>Color me a fan. <a href=3D"=
https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/7k51bxvxtPDWCXsHI_UghA~~/AABEfhA~/pdpN4vZ=
j-I5Y-RruT9jlycLUU-CyTvVUHMT9qHqme9tBOF1yV2cUalni6FbO3cVvnA1wc56j9uCRzAl8mD=
8GCPVVZdaB2wHURzoPtlyX_EHvw2sxZAOZjXBFk85MR-GNvlycul4FiyBZe70k-3ASHAHZAkRcP=
U_fy6WtiAzVwUbwpPqmBuy7_2273wdkN5yH">Tooltips are the perfect use case</a> =
for this technology combo. And a footnote can totally be a tooltip, <a href=
=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/3_J2W9g1NLrxynam4Q6Onw~~/AABEfhA~/J68=
gTBXT9ohneZ0DO60UxCgcyqi6PDQ8KbJS6sR1YdG5hg0CWP_urFWWEDNfB9Schbk1TkdvAlSR7Y=
cC29Q7KOoJlL5wjLSbIw0ccO56fCFt17xuiGV3jquz_f7BVjFCLZ5rB0PgtN_H2SFybFLvijx7A=
TjqCmgvbawnwKH58_nAgfyW7OpHMGhQqkrtEpC0">as can a menu</a>. <a href=3D"http=
s://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/bp9b-AfGpCDX0iizMp924g~~/AABEfhA~/pdpN4vZj-I5=
Y-RruT9jlyVQ1TgQIS8AygtWO-aIg9t92GnqjQaus1gq_uSjOY05p7ZYP-NPhWAWfc5dCLEfDMu=
aIxSx5KUpnnmOS99rSBoET2PUbM7HV1Sz4oo09nquDLW0xt4iUnLC7b-dyUldDBMjNJg_C1ACHm=
5KJuMljWNAzGXhEQqtSG8kHxQBEIvsH">Sidenotes</a> are nice too, if a design ca=
n pull it off.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>While we=E2=80=99re doing type stuff, Kell=
y Choyce-Dwan=E2=80=99s deep dive into balancing type across languages is e=
xcellent: <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/jpfZG4HvJP_vqxBXUSKS=
GA~~/AABEfhA~/RM_b13LT8USMGtmM_JlCaBkMVrBylNaQfD2hEy0MJ5MOtiwW9zDEPfNQvArLC=
cYGhjYEo-mxE18uBBokSAFp4NIO-jODdburbZstxmPtLlyM1vLHYkeelv9xHyk4cm7o2VLww4KA=
LKCXlmOc3f4nZzBulKP9Hsvdm8pd2eH-8Kebc_syaW3C84XnWua_TDHV">Typography troubl=
es: Balancing lines in Japanese &amp; Korean</a>. I=E2=80=99m perfectly gui=
lty of looking at new CSS tech through the lens of setting type in English =
and getting excited before understanding the non-English ramifications.</p>=
=0A<p>In this case, as nice as <code>text-wrap: balance</code> can be somet=
imes=E2=80=A6</p>=0A<blockquote><p>When working with multilingual text, lin=
e breaks don=E2=80=99t always behave as expected. For example, we added <co=
de>text-wrap: balance</code> on WordPress.org, and quickly got community fe=
edback that it led to awkward, unexpected breaks in Japanese and Korean.</p=
></blockquote>=0A<p>Which, yay, has a fancy solution using an <code>auto-ph=
rase</code> value I=E2=80=99ve literally never heard of:</p>=0A<pre><code c=
lass=3D"css">h1,=0Ah2,=0Ah3,=0Ah4,=0Ah5,=0Ah6 {=0A  text-wrap: balance;=0A=
=0A  &:lang(ja) {=0A    text-wrap: initial;=0A=0A    @supports (word-break:=
 auto-phrase) {=0A      word-break: auto-phrase;=0A      text-wrap: balance=
;=0A    }=0A  }=0A}</code></pre>=0A<p>Make sure to click over to the articl=
e for the Korean fix.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>Have you heard of =E2=80=9Cfaux bol=
d=E2=80=9D=3F Or =E2=80=9Cfaux italic=E2=80=9D=3F It=E2=80=99s this (kinda =
nasty) thing a browser will do to characters in type when you=E2=80=99ve <e=
m>asked</em> for the bold or italic version of a font, but you haven=E2=80=
=99t actually loaded that font. So the browser is like <em>fine I=E2=80=99l=
l just do it for you then</em> and it, without exception, looks like =F0=9F=
=92=A9.</p>=0A<p>The solution, if you=E2=80=99re loading up custom fonts, i=
s to ensure you=E2=80=99ve got an <code>@font-face</code> rule that covers =
those <code>font-weight</code> and <code>font-styles</code> before using th=
em. <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/M1s4ibxIbwjN-Qed7ZDV9w~~/A=
ABEfhA~/K-RJboFlNWxbk2iW0WwQU2vySAiwLTqvtTsbX4Tqy-2DZZKtvr4TFC5AfxN4G6A29do=
9jGgP1g86k0Bym3SxBCXmApm6QuUmqNlkK_5luzGTv7RK7htm2y9-lAB2WP17">Richard Rutt=
er has a good blog post on this</a>, which includes a nifty trick for detec=
ting if you=E2=80=99re accidentally doing it wrong.</p>=0A<pre><code class=
=3D"css">@keyframes flip-synthesis {=0A  0% {=0A    font-synthesis: none;=
=0A  }=0A  100% {=0A    font-synthesis: initial;=0A  }=0A}=0A=0Abody {=0A  =
animation: 3s infinite flip-synthesis;=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0D=0A           =
